Charge the pipeline refill of IRQ entry, unless a DMA hides it.

runtime_tick takes a pending, unmasked IRQ at the first instruction
boundary and calls runtime_irq, which sets the PC to the vector 0x18
without charging any cycle; only a wake from HALT adds
kIrqWakeDelayCycles. Entering an IRQ writes the PC, and mGBA charges that
write like any other: ARMRaiseIRQ does cpu->cycles += ARMWritePC(cpu),
2 cycles plus the N and S fetch waits at the vector, 2 in the zero-wait
BIOS (mGBA 0.10.5 src/arm/arm.c:123-139, include/mgba/internal/arm/
isa-inlines.h:73-81). Except while a DMA blocks the CPU: mGBA's event loop
then advances the CPU straight to its next event (GBAProcessEvents,
src/gba/gba.c:300-339, `mTimingTick(..., cycles < nextEvent ? nextEvent :
cycles)` while cpuBlocked), and the 2 cycles pass within that wait.

So in code that takes many IRQs, gbarecomp ran 2 cycles ahead of mGBA per
IRQ taken while no DMA ran, and that adds up within a frame: in a game
whose HBlank IRQ fires on every line, about 136 cycles per frame over the
68 lines of VBlank (on the visible lines the HBlank DMA hides them in
both). Enough to move a VCOUNT read across a line boundary now and then.

The change, in runtime_tick (runtime_bus_bridge.cpp): before runtime_irq,
charge 2 + runtime_refill_cycles(0x18, ARM), the same refill a generated
PC write charges, unless a DMA held the bus during the instruction that
just ran. drain_dma_steal sets a flag whenever it charges a DMA's stolen
cycles, and runtime_tick reads and clears it at every IRQ check, so the
answer does not depend on where the cycles were drained: in runtime_tick
itself (timed and FIFO DMA, or an immediate DMA started on the raw bus by
the self-heal bridge) or in runtime_resync_horizon, right after the guest
write that started an immediate DMA (generated code and the force-interp
backend). The flag is cleared when another machine's timing context is
restored. The cycles are pumped as the HALT wake delay is: devices
advance, IRQs raised meanwhile stay pending in IF, and the IRQ already
decided is taken. A wake from HALT now costs the 7 cycles of
kIrqWakeDelayCycles plus the 2 of the entry, as in mGBA (the IRQ event's
delay, then ARMRaiseIRQ). runtime_refill_cycles also empties the GamePak
prefetch buffer, as for any PC write, when the prefetch reset patch (0011)
is applied; this patch applies and builds without it, and then only adds
the cycles.

A first version hid the refill only when DMA cycles were drained in the
same runtime_tick. Generated code and the interpreter then charged it
after an immediate DMA, which they drain after the write, and the
self-heal bridge did not: comparing a commercial game's recompiled run
with its interpreter run, an IRQ taken after an 11,266-cycle DMA started
from bridged IWRAM code cost 2 cycles on one side and none on the other,
and one scanline's palette write landed on the other side of the line.

"A DMA during this instruction" approximates mGBA's "a DMA blocks the CPU
when the IRQ is raised": gbarecomp charges a DMA's stolen cycles at once,
where mGBA starts it 3 cycles after its trigger, and takes an IRQ at the
next instruction boundary, where mGBA takes it 7 cycles after it is
raised (13 for HBlank). Those latencies are not copied here, so an IRQ
raised in the last few cycles of a DMA is hidden here and not in mGBA.
Multiplayer session execution takes IRQs in runtime_should_yield, not
here, and is unchanged; so is tools/bios_smoke, which keeps its own IRQ
loop.

Tested on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21) at gbarecomp 5e89943 plus this
series:

- gbarecomp's ctest, 52 of 52 (with the gba-tests test ROMs).
- Two synthetic 4 KiB ROMs (no Nintendo data, header logo zero), on a
  minimal stand-in BIOS (vectors, an IRQ dispatcher, VBlankIntrWait) and
  on the retail BIOS, each on generated code (GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC=1,
  fully static) and on the interpreter (GBARECOMP_FORCE_INTERP=1, BIOS
  interpreted too). irqentry1 times 100 iterations of `subs; bne` from the
  cartridge between two timer 0 reads at F/1, alone and with one timer 1
  IRQ inside (no DMA, the handler in the cartridge): mGBA 0.10.5 gives
  2596 and 2748 cycles on the stand-in BIOS (2747 on the retail one);
  gbarecomp 5e89943 2596 and 2746 on both backends; with this patch,
  mGBA's values on both. irqentry2 times one immediate DMA3 of 64 words
  from EWRAM to EWRAM, started by a store between the two timer reads,
  alone and with the timer 1 IRQ raised while it runs: mGBA gives 789 and
  939 (938 on the retail BIOS), as does gbarecomp with this patch and
  without it on both backends; the first version gave 941. The generators
  and configs are in tests/repro/ of
  https://github.com/nvergez/moto-racer-advanced-browser (make_rom.py,
  make_bios.py, irqentry1.toml, irqentry2.toml); attach them when filing
  this patch.
- A commercial game (HBlank IRQ on every line, HBlank DMA on the visible
  lines), on the interpreter, with the prefetch reset (0011 and its
  arm-recomp-core half): over one race frame, the IRQ entries on VBlank
  lines cost 2 cycles in mGBA (58 of 60 measured) and 0 without this
  patch, 2 (57 of 60) with it; on visible lines 30 in mGBA (158 of 160)
  and with this patch (155 of 160). Without it the game's random number
  generator, which adds VCOUNT to its state, read one line earlier than in
  mGBA at the first draw near a line boundary, and the race diverged; with
  it, the generator's state equals mGBA's on every frame of a full race,
  and a three-race cup ends with mGBA's positions and points.

